The '''2021 Carretera Gran Presa del Nord protest''' was a sit-in demonstration held on 8 March 2021 at the construction site of the Nou Gran Presa Carretera — a proposed road that would connect the [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]] to the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] — in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca of [[Tramuntana Province]], [[Balboa]]. The protest was organised by the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB) and local [[Birú people|Birú]] community assemblies in opposition to the road's planned route, which would require a tunnel through a limestone formation containing caves that are sacred to the Birú people. The protest ended in violence when a confrontation between protesters and private security guards employed by [[Nivell de Seguretat]] resulted in the disappearance and subsequent death of a protester, [[Hernandez Lee]].

The kidnapping and killing of Lee, who was last seen in the custody of Nivell de Seguretat guard [[Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal]], triggered a national media controversy and led to the suspension of the road project. The project was subsequently redesigned with a revised route that avoided the Birú sacred caves, following consultation with Birú community representatives.

== Background ==

The Nou Gran Presa Carretera was a proposed road project to improve access between the [[E8 expressway (Balboa)|E8 expressway]] and the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] hydroelectric facility on the southern shore of [[Lake Iwarakí]]. The existing access road to the dam was narrow, winding, and frequently impassable during the wet season, and the government had identified an upgraded road connection as a priority for the maintenance and operation of the dam's hydroelectric infrastructure.

The proposed route of the Nou Gran Presa Carretera passed through the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca, a region with a significant [[Birú people|Birú]] population and a history of conflict over infrastructure development. The route required a tunnel through a limestone formation on the eastern slope of the Yalabirga range, which contained a network of caves that have been used by the Birú for ceremonial purposes for centuries. The caves are regarded as sacred sites in Birú cosmology and are among the most significant indigenous cultural sites in the Tramuntana highlands.

The Birú communities in the area, organised through the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB), had opposed the road project since its announcement in 2020. The MPB argued that the tunnel would damage the structural integrity of the cave system, disrupt access to the ceremonial sites, and desecrate a place of profound spiritual significance. The movement called for the road to be rerouted to avoid the caves entirely and for the Birú communities to be consulted on any infrastructure project affecting their traditional lands.

The government and the [[Corporació Hidroelèctrica de Balboa]] (CHB), which operated the Gran Presa del Nord, argued that the road was essential for the dam's maintenance and that the proposed tunnel route was the most technically and economically feasible option. An environmental impact assessment had been completed in 2020, but the assessment had not included a specific evaluation of the caves' cultural significance, and Birú representatives had not been consulted during the planning process.

== Protest ==

On the morning of 8 March 2021, approximately 200 protesters gathered at the construction site of the Nou Gran Presa Carretera, where preliminary clearing and surveying work had begun. The protest was organised by the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] (MPB) and local Birú community assemblies, and participants included Birú community members, MPB activists, university students, and environmentalists.

The protesters established a sit-in across the access road to the construction site, blocking the movement of construction equipment and personnel. The demonstration was peaceful in its initial hours, with participants holding banners, chanting, and performing traditional Birú ceremonies at the site.

[[Nivell de Seguretat]] (NdS), which had been contracted by the construction company to provide site security, deployed a team of approximately 15 guards to the site. The guards were instructed to prevent protesters from entering the construction zone and to protect the equipment. Tensions between the protesters and the security guards escalated throughout the morning as the guards attempted to clear the access road.

=== Confrontation ===

In the early afternoon, a group of guards attempted to physically remove protesters from the access road. During the ensuing confrontation, several protesters and guards engaged in physical altercations. Among them were [[Hernandez Lee]], a 23-year-old Zong-Balboan protester and MPB member, and [[Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal]], a 34-year-old NdS security guard.

Witnesses reported that Lee and Mendosas engaged in a prolonged physical fight, which was broken up by other protesters and guards. Following the altercation, the protesters regrouped and the sit-in continued. The confrontation was reported to the local police, but no arrests were made at the scene.

== Kidnapping and death of Hernandez Lee ==

{{main|Hernandez Lee|Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal}}

After the protest concluded in the late afternoon, Hernandez Lee departed the site on foot, intending to walk to [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]], approximately 12 kilometres to the north, where he was staying with friends. Lee was last seen by fellow protesters at approximately 17:30, walking alone along the E8 expressway.

According to the subsequent police investigation, Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal left the construction site in a NdS vehicle after the protest concluded and followed Lee along the E8. Mendosas intercepted Lee at a point where the expressway passed through a remote, uninhabited stretch of highland terrain. Mendosas forced Lee into the vehicle and drove him to an isolated location in the highlands east of the construction site.

Lee was reported missing by his friends in Sant Jorge on the morning of 9 March when he failed to arrive. The [[National Police of Balboa]] launched a search operation on 9 March. Mendosas was identified as a suspect after witnesses at the protest reported the altercation between him and Lee, and after NdS vehicle records showed that his vehicle had left the construction site after the protest.

Mendosas was arrested on 10 March 2021. Lee's body was discovered on 24 March 2021 in a wooded area approximately 8 kilometres east of the construction site. The autopsy determined that the cause of death was blunt force trauma to the head and torso, consistent with a beating. Mendosas was charged with kidnapping and murder.

== Aftermath ==

=== Legal proceedings ===

{{main|Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal}}

Jonaes Mendosas i Vidal was tried before the Tramuntana Provincial Court in 2022. The prosecution presented evidence that Mendosas had left the construction site in a NdS vehicle after the protest, had followed Lee along the E8 expressway, and had driven him to a remote location where he was killed. Vehicle tracking data, witness testimony, and forensic evidence placed Mendosas at the scene of the kidnapping and the site where the body was discovered. Mendosas was convicted of kidnapping and murder and sentenced to life imprisonment.

The family of Hernandez Lee filed a civil lawsuit against Nivell de Seguretat in 2021, alleging that the company was liable for Mendosas's actions under the doctrine of {{w|vicarious liability}}. The family argued that NdS had failed to properly screen, train, and supervise its security personnel, and that the company's deployment of guards to the protest site had created the conditions for the confrontation and subsequent kidnapping.

Nivell de Seguretat contested the lawsuit, arguing that Mendosas had acted outside the scope of his employment and without the knowledge or authorisation of the company. The company maintained that its guards were instructed to maintain order at the construction site and that Mendosas's actions — following Lee after the protest had concluded and driving him to a remote location — were a personal criminal act unrelated to his duties as a security guard. The company also noted that Mendosas had used a company vehicle without authorisation and that his actions were a violation of company policy.

In 2023, the court found Nivell de Seguretat not liable for the death of Hernandez Lee, ruling that Mendosas's actions were outside the scope of his employment and that the company had not been negligent in its hiring or training practices. The ruling was controversial and was criticised by the [[Moviment del poble Birú]] and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB), who argued that the company should bear responsibility for the actions of its employees.

=== Project suspension and redesign ===

The kidnapping and killing of Hernandez Lee and the subsequent media coverage prompted the government to suspend construction of the Nou Gran Presa Carretera on 12 March 2021. The suspension was announced by the Ministry of Infrastructure, which stated that the project would be reviewed in light of the events at the protest site and the concerns raised by Birú communities.

A revised route for the road was approved in 2023 following consultation with Birú community representatives. The revised route avoids the limestone formation containing the sacred caves entirely, running to the west of the original alignment through an area of previously cleared land. The Birú communities participated in the planning of the revised route and endorsed the project. Construction of the revised road began in 2024.

